  "noteTemplates": {
    "floor": "Below Rails {rails}'s required_ruby_version (>= {minRuby})",
    "kwarg": "Ruby 3.0's positional/keyword-argument separation breaks Rails {rails} outright (confirmed, e.g. <a href=\"https://github.com/rails/rails/issues/40938\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rails/rails#40938</a>)",
    "green-conf": "Ruby {ruby} was already out (or came out within a year) when Rails {rails} shipped — within the range Rails actually tests and tracks in its CI.",
    "green-inf": "Ruby {ruby} arrived ~{years} years into Rails {rails}'s life. Likely still within its maintenance window, but not confirmed against a specific Rails CI run — treat as a best-effort inference.",
    "blue-inf": "Ruby {ruby} shipped well after Rails {rails} was EOL'd; commonly reported to mostly work, but never an officially tested pairing — treat as a best-effort inference.",
    "workaround": "Runs only with a workaround: Ruby {ruby}'s built-in Set class and (from 3.4 on) removal of default gems like cgi clash with Rails {rails} internals until the fixes backported in 7.1+ (see <a href=\"https://github.com/rails/rails/pull/55037\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rails/rails#55037</a>). Add the Ruby stdlib gems that 3.4+ dropped from the default set (cgi, benchmark, mutex_m, drb, etc.) as explicit Gemfile dependencies, and require \"set\" at the very top of config/boot.rb before Rails loads, so Ruby's built-in Set class is in place before ActiveSupport tries to patch it."
  },
